## Phase 5: Migrate layered status artwork

Depends on Phase 4. Outcome: native composition supports custom layered effects without taking over gameplay logic.

    ☐ Migrate player wetness, body temperature, and composite weather, including their text-only styles.
        ☐ Wetness: preserve filled drops, percentage fitting and current footprint, text-above order, and backdrop alignment.
        ☐ Body temperature: compose reading, trend markers, narrow thermometer, and backdrop through native bounds; retain fill masking, clipping, tint, optional readings, and warming/cooling marker semantics.
        ☐ Weather: retain independent layer selection, shared origins, draw order, complete-pack/fallback behavior, and borrowed textures.
    ☐ Keep custom elements focused on their visual responsibility. Do not port entire old presentation routines unchanged into callback elements.
    ☐ Test optional components, simultaneous layers, trend strengths, clipping, native bounds, scale changes, and owned/borrowed cleanup against existing behavioral relationships.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ All three consumers render from prepared native component geometry; masking and animation do not trigger component layout.

## Phase 6: Migrate environmental instruments and compact styles

Depends on Phase 5. Outcome: every built-in style uses native presentations.

    ☐ Migrate approximate time, environmental temperature, and calendar date across immersive, icon, and text styles.
    ☐ Replace compact HudOverlayIconReading arrangements with native icon/reading components, retaining absent-reading behavior and localized measurements.
    ☐ Compose instrument layers and registered reading/mechanism regions through parented native bounds. Retain authored canvases, aspect ratios, scales, calibration, clock segments/pointer, thermometer reveal, and mechanical layer order.
    ☐ Keep native dynamic elements responsible for prepared instrument artwork and visual animation; updates must not resample gameplay or rearrange components during rendering.
    ☐ Verify style changes preserve feature state and timing while releasing superseded GUI resources and invalidating only the necessary measurement/composition work.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ The Phase 1 consumer/style inventory is fully migrated; no built-in style relies on full-indicator manual Draw dispatch.

## Phase 7: Retire superseded presentation infrastructure and document the boundary

Depends on Phase 6. Outcome: the native architecture is the sole built-in presentation path.

    ☐ Remove HudIndicatorStackLayout and obsolete group-wide built-in dispatch, manual component absolute-bounds assignment, and presentation helpers whose responsibilities have been replaced.
        ☐ Preserve necessary framebuffer/scissor boundaries and custom artwork calculations; do not confuse those with component layout.
        ☐ Remove superseded helpers and dispatch paths once their consumers are migrated; no external compatibility facade is required.
    ☐ Reconcile resource owners, removal handles, style switches, exit completion, and world lifecycle for double disposal, stale visual state, or obsolete callbacks.
    ☐ Update HudOverlays.md and public integration examples to describe the current native capability, element/composer ownership and invalidation.
    ☐ Update affected feature documentation where its presentation implementation changed; permanent documentation must describe active code rather than refactor history.
    ☐ Search source and linked documentation for stale claims, manual drawing fallbacks, render-time layout calls, and orphaned infrastructure/tests. Reconcile every remaining match against its intended responsibility.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Cleanup is complete and documentation matches the resulting implementation.

## Phase 8: Qualify the complete implementation

Depends on Phase 7. Outcome: the final candidate has automated, package, and review evidence without implying visual acceptance.

    ☐ Reconcile every approved proposal requirement against implementation and focused evidence; inspect all ten consumers and every supported style.
    ☐ Verify repeated native rendering performs no arrangement, fitting, measurement, bounds calculation, or resource creation for unchanged content. Test relevant invalidations separately from content-only and animation-only changes.
    ☐ Verify parent/child relationships, scaling, containment, centering, optional rows, clipping, text-above order, backdrop alignment, passive input, fades, and animation continuity.
    ☐ Exercise grouping, movement, resize, GUI-scale/style changes, hidden proxies, previews/cancellation, saved layouts/locks, disabled content, removal, failed initialization, and repeated world transitions.
    ☐ Verify actual native element registration and exactly-once ownership across regrouping/previews.
    ☐ Run appropriate affected tests and the canonical Release build/package through subagents, as required by the user's instructions. Inspect package contents and verify relevant assets and DLL identity against the qualified candidate.
        ☐ Follow Tests/AGENTS.md: test relationships, supplied geometry, caching, invalidation, ordering, and lifecycle; do not lock tests to prescribed visual sizes, palettes, fonts, or pixels.
        ☐ Distinguish source inspection, headless native integration, package identity, skips, and any remaining runtime evidence. Do not present them as measured performance or appearance acceptance.
    ☐ Complete a second implementation review and independent completion audit. Resolve findings against the whole proposal/plan before recording completion.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Required automated checks, package verification, documentation reconciliation, and reviews pass on the final candidate; remaining visual/input acceptance stays explicitly open.

## Phase 9: User-run in-game acceptance

Depends on Phase 8. Outcome: rendered appearance and native interaction have been checked by the user.

    ☐ Provide the qualified package and a concise user-run checklist. Do not launch the game on the user's behalf.
    ☐ Obtain user results for representative indicators in every supported style, normal/high GUI scales, resizing, text updates, optional readings, layer ordering, instrument animation, and visibility transitions.
    ☐ Obtain user results for passive input, dialogs/tooltips, editing, grouping, movement, preview cancellation, hidden-item proxies, persisted locks/layouts, and world exit/rejoin.
    ☐ Resolve reported discrepancies within scope, rerun checks appropriate to any changes, and obtain renewed user acceptance where appearance/input changed.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ User-run acceptance is recorded separately from automated results. Unperformed checks remain unchecked.

## Final acceptance

    ☐ Every proposal requirement is satisfied, or a scope change has explicit user approval; implementation convenience is not an implicit deferral.
    ☐ All built-in presentations are actual native GuiElements with composer-owned lifetimes and layout resolved before rendering.
    ☐ Superseded internal layout/dispatch paths are retired; no compatibility adapter remains.
    ☐ Automated qualification, package verification, documentation, reviews, and user-run acceptance are complete with truthful evidence and no unsupported performance claims.

## Proposal coverage

- Proposed design: Phases 1–6 establish native components, retained controller responsibilities, and complete built-in/style migration.
- Update and rendering contract: Phases 1, 3–6, and 8 establish invalidation and prove rendering consumes prepared geometry; Phase 7 removes superseded machinery.
- Ownership and compatibility: Phases 1–3, 7, and 8 cover native capability, composer ownership, previews, lifecycle, and preserved user state.
- Acceptance: Phases 8–9 distinguish automated/package evidence from user-run visual and input acceptance.
